//
// Expected: a0 — holding the key is not enough to defeat an AEAD check.
//
// `k` is leaked, so the attacker holds the decryption key. `ad` is a secret
// Alice and Bob share and never send, so the attacker cannot produce any
// AEAD_ENC(k, ., ad): the tag is bound to the associated data. Bob halts on
// every substitution of `c`, and there is no attack.
//
// The guard bypass used to fire on the key alone, and reported a1 with the
// trace saying "Attacker holds k, so it can supply a value this check
// accepts" -- which the engine's own derivability judgement would have
// refused. It now also requires every other position the rewrite rule pins,
// which for AEAD_DEC is the associated data.
//
// bypass_needs_the_whole_check_ad_leaked.vp is the counterweight: leak `ad`
// too and the forgery really is constructible, so the a1 comes back.
// The nonce on `n_c` is declared rather than generated so the ciphertext
// stays the same value in every session, as it was before nonces were named:
// generated per run it would add a cross-session duplicate acceptance at two
// sessions, which is not what this model is about.
attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows private ad
	leaks k
	knows private plain
	knows private n_c
	c = AEAD_ENC(k, n_c, plain, ad)
]

Alice -> Bob: n_c, c

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	knows private ad
	m = AEAD_DEC(k, n_c, c, ad)?
	generates s
	out = HASH(s, m)
]

Bob -> Alice: out

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: c
]
